const MUSEUM = [{"a":"The Rosetta Stone","al":["the rosetta stone","rosetta stone","rosetta"],"c":["A slab of granodiorite about 112 centimeters tall, carrying one message in three scripts.","Soldiers of a French expedition dug it up in 1799, while rebuilding a fort in the Nile Delta.","Its decree, issued at Memphis in 196 BC, appears in hieroglyphic, Demotic, and Greek.","Champollion used it in 1822 to crack the hieroglyphs after fourteen centuries of silence.","It is the British Museum’s most visited object, and shorthand for the key to any puzzle."],"w":"Three scripts, one message: without it, Egypt’s written past stays sealed."},{"a":"The Mask of Tutankhamun","al":["mask of tutankhamun","tutankhamun mask","tutankhamuns mask","tutankhamun","king tut mask","tut mask"],"c":["Ten kilos of gold, inlaid with lapis lazuli, quartz, and obsidian.","It was fashioned around 1323 BC for a king who died before his twentieth year.","Howard Carter’s crew opened its case in 1922 and saw “wonderful things” by candlelight.","The striped headdress bears a cobra and a vulture — the Two Ladies of Egypt.","The face of the boy king’s tomb, and of a whole lost dynasty."],"w":"The boy king was a footnote of history; the mask made him immortal."},{"a":"The Antikythera Mechanism","al":["antikythera mechanism","antikythera","antikythera device"],"c":["Thirty-odd bronze gears, corroded and split, pulled from a Roman-era shipwreck in 1901.","Nothing else like it is known for the next fourteen centuries.","Its hand-cranked dials tracked the sun, the moon, and the planets through the zodiac.","It computed the Metonic cycle, predicted eclipses, and even marked the Olympiad games.","Often called the world’s first computer — a Greek astronomical machine from around 100 BC."],"w":"Someone built a planetarium in bronze a thousand years before clockwork existed anywhere else."},{"a":"The Dead Sea Scrolls","al":["dead sea scrolls","dead sea scroll","qumran scrolls"],"c":["A shepherd chasing a stray goat threw a stone into a Judean cave and heard pottery break — so one account goes.","Eleven caves near Qumran gave up fragments of some 900 manuscripts between 1946 and 1956.","Most were written on parchment in Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek, some two thousand years ago.","They include the oldest known copies of the Hebrew Bible, a millennium older than anything before them.","Today most of them rest in the Shrine of the Book in Jerusalem."],"w":"Every modern Bible translation leans, at some verse, on a cave near the Dead Sea."},{"a":"The Venus de Milo","al":["venus de milo","venus of milo","aphrodite of melos"],"c":["Parian marble, about two meters tall, missing both arms — the holes for her bracelets survive.","A farmer unearthed her in 1820 on the Aegean island of Melos.","She was carved around 100 BC, a blend of Classical ideals and Hellenistic drama.","She may have held an apple — melos means apple — or a shield, or a spindle.","The Louvre’s armless goddess, sold to France within months of her discovery."],"w":"Two arms are missing; the argument about them never ends."},{"a":"The Terracotta Army","al":["terracotta army","terracotta warriors","terracotta soldiers"],"c":["Local well-diggers near Xi’an struck a head of hardened clay in 1974.","An estimated 8,000 soldiers stand in the ranks — and no two faces are alike.","They were painted in bright lacquers that flaked within minutes of excavation.","Each carries real bronze weapons, some still sharp after 2,200 years.","They guard the tomb of Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor of China."],"w":"One emperor, one afterlife army — bureaucracy rendered in clay."},{"a":"The Sutton Hoo Helmet","al":["sutton hoo helmet","sutton hoo"],"c":["Iron plates, tinned bronze, and garnets — smashed into some 500 pieces and buried in a ship.","It was dug from an East Anglian mound in 1939, on the eve of war.","The face-mask has eyebrows ending in gilded boars, and a dragon flies across the crest.","The 27-meter ship beneath the mound rotted away, leaving only its ghost pressed in sand.","The crowning find of Anglo-Saxon England, probably the grave-goods of King Raedwald."],"w":"An entire ship dissolved to a shadow in the sand; the helmet refused."},{"a":"The Bayeux Tapestry","al":["bayeux tapestry","bayeux"],"c":["Not a tapestry at all: seventy meters of embroidered wool on linen.","Its Latin captions run from 1064 to 1066 and stop mid-scene.","A famous comet streaks across one sky, above frightened courtiers.","It ends with the fall of the housecarls on the ridge near Hastings.","The Normans’ own picture-book of the conquest of England."],"w":"The winners embroidered their version, and it outlasted every chronicle."},{"a":"The Lewis Chessmen","al":["lewis chessmen","lewis chess set","lewis chess pieces"],"c":["Ninety-three pieces of walrus ivory and whale tooth, found in a sand dune in 1831.","Most now sit in the British Museum; Edinburgh holds the rest.","The queens rest their cheeks on their hands; the berserkers bite their shields.","They were carved in Norway around 1150, likely for trade to the Norse isles.","The most famous chess set of the Middle Ages — from the Isle of Lewis in the Hebrides."],"w":"Vikings played the same endgames we do."},{"a":"The Nefertiti Bust","al":["nefertiti bust","nefertiti"],"c":["A painted limestone core under a coat of stucco, about 47 centimeters tall.","One eye was never finished — a teaching piece, or something stranger.","Ludwig Borchardt’s expedition dug her from a sculptor’s studio at Amarna in 1912.","Her name means “the beautiful one has come,” and her blue crown is like no other queen’s.","She has watched over Berlin for a century, while Egypt keeps asking her home."],"w":"A portrait so perfect it became a diplomatic problem."},{"a":"Ötzi the Iceman","al":["otzi","iceman","otzi the iceman","the iceman","otzi the ice mummy"],"c":["Two German hikers found a body melting out of a glacier in 1991 — and assumed it was a modern death.","He carried 61 tattoos, an axe of nearly pure copper, and shoes of bearskin and deer hide.","His last meals were ibex and red deer, einkorn bread, and bracken — his final day was busy.","An arrowhead in his left shoulder, and traces of four others’ blood on his blade, told of a violent end.","Europe’s oldest natural human mummy, 5,300 years old, now rests in Bolzano, Italy."],"w":"Five thousand years later, we know what he ate for breakfast — and who he fought at the end."},{"a":"The Mona Lisa","al":["mona lisa","la gioconda","la joconde"],"c":["A poplar panel, 77 by 53 centimeters — smaller than most hardcover books.","It was never delivered: the artist carried it from Florence toward France, adjusting it for years.","The sitter was Lisa Gherardini, wife of a silk merchant.","In 1911 a handyman walked out of the Louvre with it under his smock; Picasso was briefly a suspect.","Leonardo’s portrait with the famous smile — the most visited painting on earth."],"w":"The most famous smile in the world was a commission portrait."},{"a":"The Starry Night","al":["the starry night","starry night"],"c":["A canvas painted from memory, looking east before sunrise through a barred window.","The village below is invented; the cypress in the foreground is real.","It was painted in June 1889, from a quiet asylum at Saint-Rémy-de-Provence.","Eleven stars and a wavy moon swirl above a landscape the artist never saw so calmly again.","Van Gogh’s swirling night over Provence — an icon of the Museum of Modern Art since 1941."],"w":"Painted from an asylum window, and brighter than anything in the sky outside it."},{"a":"Guernica","al":["guernica"],"c":["A bull, a horse, a light bulb shaped like an eye, a weeping mother — all in black, white, and grey.","It was painted in about three weeks of fury in a Paris studio, in May 1937.","A tapestry copy hangs at the United Nations, where world leaders file past it.","It answered a market town bombed on a Monday, by planes of the Condor Legion.","Picasso’s enormous anti-war painting of a Basque town’s destruction, now at the Reina Sofía."],"w":"Commissioned for a pavilion, it grew too large for any war but the one it showed."},{"a":"The Liberty Bell","al":["liberty bell"],"c":["Cast twice, because the first one cracked on its first test ring.","It carries a line from Leviticus: “Proclaim liberty throughout all the land.”","The famous crack grew through decades of ringing, until 1846.","It rang — or so the story goes — for the first public reading of the Declaration of Independence.","Philadelphia’s cracked bell, moved indoors from Independence Hall in 1976."],"w":"A bell too cracked to ring, and impossible to retire."},{"a":"The Wright Flyer","al":["wright flyer","wright flyer 1","the flyer","1903 flyer","wright brothers plane","wright plane"],"c":["Spruce and muslin, a twelve-horsepower engine, and a landing gear of skids.","It cost about a thousand dollars and had no wheels — it launched from a rail.","Its longest flight on the first day lasted 59 seconds, into a cold December wind.","The pilot lay prone beside the engine; roll control came from warping the wingtips.","The 1903 Kitty Hawk machine — the first powered airplane, now hanging at the Smithsonian."],"w":"Twelve horsepower and twelve seconds of flight, and the century turned over."},{"a":"The Apollo 11 Command Module","al":["apollo 11 command module","columbia command module","columbia capsule","apollo command module"],"c":["A cone of steel and honeycomb: three couches, five windows, and a heat shield.","While two men walked on the Moon, its pilot photographed the landing site and waited alone for 21 hours.","It splashed down in the Pacific southwest of Hawaii on July 24, 1969, and went straight to quarantine.","Its name was picked at nearly the last minute — after the island of the Statue of Liberty.","Columbia, the command module of Apollo 11, now rests in Washington, D.C."],"w":"One astronaut kept the ship while the whole world looked at the Moon."},{"a":"The Code of Hammurabi","al":["code of hammurabi","hammurabi code","hammurabis code","stele of hammurabi"],"c":["A black diorite pillar, 2.25 meters tall, carved with about 4,000 lines of cuneiform.","It was carted off to Susa as war booty around 1150 BC, and dug up again in 1901.","Its prologue says the gods called a king “to cause justice to prevail in the world.”","Law 196 is famous, and grim: an eye for an eye.","Hammurabi’s law code of Babylon, from around 1754 BC — in the Louvre."],"w":"Justice so old it carved the punishments into stone where nobody could soften them."},{"a":"The Vasa","al":["the vasa","vasa","vasa ship"],"c":["Sixty-four bronze guns, two decks, and a stern covered in painted lions and Greek heroes.","It sailed about 1,300 meters on its maiden voyage — then heeled, flooded, and sank.","The 1628 disaster killed around thirty people; the inquest blamed nobody by name.","It sat in Stockholm harbor for 333 years, until a wreck-hunter raised it in 1961, nearly intact.","Sweden’s top-heavy royal warship, now the centerpiece of its own museum."],"w":"A ship that sank in front of half of Stockholm, then waited for them to come back."},{"a":"The Hope Diamond","al":["hope diamond"],"c":["A 45.5-carat blue stone whose legend keeps accumulating deaths it never caused.","It was cut from the “French Blue,” stolen from the Crown Jewels in 1792.","It glows red under ultraviolet light, phosphorescing for minutes after the lamp goes off.","Its owners’ misfortunes — the newspapers said — ran from a beheaded queen to a sunk liner; the records mostly say otherwise.","The deep-blue diamond mailed to the Smithsonian in a plain brown wrapper, in 1958."],"w":"A rock with better publicists than geologists."},{"a":"Michelangelo’s David","al":["david","michelangelos david","michelangelo david","the david"],"c":["A giant block of Carrara marble, abandoned for decades after two sculptors botched it.","It stands 5.17 meters, its head and right hand enlarged on purpose — for the view from below.","The sling is over the shoulder and the stone hides in the left hand: ambush, not duel.","It took its place outside the Palazzo Vecchio in Florence in 1504, a symbol of the republic.","Michelangelo’s shepherd-giant, now in the Accademia gallery."],"w":"A block two sculptors ruined, and a 26-year-old who saw the shepherd inside."},{"a":"The Magna Carta","al":["magna carta","magna charta","the great charter"],"c":["A single sheet of calfskin, close to A4, in abbreviated Latin — sealed, not signed.","It was annulled by the Pope within ten weeks of Runnymede.","A third of its 63 clauses are about forests; only a few lines of it remain in law today.","“No free man shall be taken or imprisoned...” — clause 39, a seed of due process.","King John’s 1215 Great Charter, the grandfather of constitutional law."],"w":"A peace treaty that failed in ten weeks and outlived every king who signed it."},{"a":"The Moai of Easter Island","al":["moai","easter island heads","easter island statues","moai statues"],"c":["Nearly a thousand of them, most with bodies buried, ring a remote Pacific island.","They stand with their backs to the sea, eyes once inlaid with coral and obsidian.","The heaviest moved weighs over 80 tons, by a method still argued over — walked upright, some say.","They were carved from a single quarry at Rano Raraku between about 1250 and 1500.","The stone heads of Rapa Nui — Easter Island."],"w":"Faces for the ancestors, carved until the island ran out of trees to move them."},{"a":"The Piltdown Man","al":["piltdown man","piltdown"],"c":["A gravel pit in Sussex gave up skull fragments and a jaw in 1912: England’s first “early man.”","The jaw was stained, the teeth filed flat — quiet doubts simmered for four decades.","He solved an awkward problem: why had England no early humans when France and Germany had plenty?","Fluorine dating raised the flag in 1949; by 1953 he was exposed as a patchwork fake.","The most famous scientific forgery of the century: a medieval human skull and an ape’s jaw."],"w":"England wanted an ancestor, so somebody arranged one."},{"a":"The Gutenberg Bible","al":["gutenberg bible","the gutenberg bible","42-line bible","the 42-line bible","gutenberg"],"c":["No single author: a Mainz goldsmith in debt squeezed out about 180 copies in the 1450s, and some 49 survive.","Its two volumes hold 42 lines of type to the page, inked by a press rather than a scribe’s hand.","Copies on paper and on vellum were finished by hand, the red headings brushed in after the press was done.","The first major book of the Western printing revolution, it broke the scriptoria’s thousand-year monopoly.","It carries the name of Johannes Gutenberg, whose workshop launched the age of the printed page."],"w":"Before it, a book took a scribe a season; after it, a press could make hundreds. Reading, and history itself, never moved the same way again."},{"a":"The Cyrus Cylinder","al":["cyrus cylinder","the cyrus cylinder","cyrus"],"c":["A barrel of baked clay about 23 centimeters long, covered in cramped little wedges.","The wedges are Babylonian cuneiform, composed in the style of that city’s own royal inscriptions.","It celebrates a conquest won without a battle: Babylon fell to a Persian king in October 539 BCE.","The new ruler let deported peoples go home, which is why one biblical book remembers him kindly.","Written for Cyrus the Great, dug from Babylon’s ruins in 1879, it now keeps quiet in the British Museum."],"w":"Conquest, propaganda, and a kinder empire than most: an imperial press release in clay, from 539 BCE."},{"a":"The Mask of Agamemnon","al":["mask of agamemnon","the mask of agamemnon","agamemnon mask","agamemnon"],"c":["A gold funeral face about 30 centimeters tall, beaten from a single sheet with chased detail.","In 1876 it came out of a shaft grave at Mycenae, dug by a German businessman with a gift for timing.","The eyes are slit shut, the mouth is calm, and the hair is braided into rows of tiny curls.","The finder reportedly wired his king that he had gazed upon the face of Agamemnon, and the name never came off.","It is centuries older than the Trojan War of Homer, and whispers of tampering have followed it since the day it surfaced."],"w":"Schliemann’s timing was perfect and his names were not: the mask is royal, Mycenaean, and about 3,500 years old, but Agamemnon it is not."},{"a":"The Book of Kells","al":["book of kells","the book of kells","kells"],"c":["Four gospels in 340 leaves, made by monks around 800 CE, probably begun on Iona and finished at Kells in Ireland.","Whole pages are surrendered to ornament: animals, knots, and tangled letters teem in colors ground from distant ores.","Viking raids scattered the community that began it, and in 1007 a thief stole its greatest volume from the abbey church for its golden cover.","The book came back within months, its cover gone, and it has been a celebrity of Irish Christianity ever since.","It has lived in the library of Trinity College Dublin since the 1660s, where visitors queue past its open pages."],"w":"Ireland’s monks called it the great relic of Columba. Call it the most extravagantly decorated book that survives, and few will argue."},{"a":"The Portland Vase","al":["portland vase","the portland vase"],"c":["A two-layer Roman glass: cobalt blue beneath a crust of white, cut away like a cameo to leave figures afloat.","It was made about two thousand years ago, and the scene, gods or lovers or both, has never been settled.","By tradition it was found in an ancient marble coffin near Rome and passed through the palaces of cardinals.","In 1845 a visitor hurled a nearby object at it in the British Museum and smashed it to pieces; it was restored from the shards.","Its name comes from a duke of Portland, and it has stood in the British Museum since 1810."],"w":"Two millennia of survival, undone in seconds by one man on a spree, then reassembled from a basket of shards."},{"a":"The Royal Game of Ur","al":["royal game of ur","the royal game of ur","game of ur","ur"],"c":["A racing board of twenty squares, its wood inlaid with shell, red limestone, and lapis lazuli.","Players threw tetrahedral dice and raced pieces home, landing on rosette squares for an extra throw.","It was buried in the royal cemetery of Ur around 2600 BCE, among the tombs Leonard Woolley opened in the 1920s.","Its rules survived on a Babylonian clay tablet, decoded in the 1980s by the British Museum’s Irving Finkel.","One of the boards lives in London and a sister in Philadelphia, and both are still perfectly playable."],"w":"The oldest board game we can still play by its own written rules: five thousand years old, and backgammon is its descendant."},{"a":"The Lamassu","al":["lamassu","the lamassu","winged bull","the winged bull","lamassu of nimrud"],"c":["A guardian with a bull’s body, eagle’s wings, and a crowned man’s face, worked in five legs.","From the front it stands still; from the side it strides. The fifth leg makes both true at once.","Between its legs, cuneiform credits Ashurnasirpal II with the palace gate it held in the ninth century BCE.","Four meters of gypsum, weighing tonnes, it guarded Nimrud for an Assyrian king who called himself king of the world.","The ones that stayed in Iraq were sledgehammered by ISIS in 2015; the others stand guard in London, Paris, and Chicago."],"w":"An empire’s doorman, three thousand years on the job, now guarding museum halls instead of kings."},{"a":"The Phaistos Disc","al":["phaistos disc","the phaistos disc","phaistos disk","the phaistos disk","disc of phaistos"],"c":["A disc of fired clay about 15 centimeters across, stamped on both faces with 241 crisp marks.","The 45 different signs run in a spiral from rim to center, pressed with ready-made seals rather than written by hand.","A Cretan palace gave it up in 1908, in a layer that dates it near 1700 BCE.","Because every mark was stamped, some call it the oldest printed text on Earth, and it certainly is the strangest.","Dozens of scholars have claimed to read it in as many languages, and none of the claims has convinced the others."],"w":"The world’s most famous unreadable sentence: 45 stamps, one spiral, and thirty-seven centuries of silence."},{"a":"The Staffordshire Hoard","al":["staffordshire hoard","the staffordshire hoard"],"c":["In July 2009 a metal detectorist in a Midlands field heard a signal and started digging into gold.","Out came more than four thousand pieces, nearly all war gear: sword fittings, helmet fragments, and biblical scenes in gold and garnet.","There was not a single coin in the hoard, which is one of its genuine mysteries.","It went into the ground around 650 CE, in the age of Mercia’s warrior kings, and nobody ever came back for it.","The find was valued at 3.28 million pounds, split between finder and landowner, and raised by public appeal in a matter of weeks."],"w":"Four kilograms of warrior gold, hidden in a field for thirteen centuries, and not a word anywhere about why."},{"a":"The Venus of Willendorf","al":["venus of willendorf","the venus of willendorf","willendorf","the willendorf venus"],"c":["Eleven centimeters of limestone, carved while the Ice Age held Europe, about 30,000 years ago.","Heavy hips, folded arms, and braid upon braid of hair, all worked with care, and a face left entirely blank.","Traces of red ochre still ride her surface, the meaning of which went unrecorded.","She was dug from a terrace above the Danube in 1908, near the Austrian village of Willendorf.","She keeps house in Vienna’s Natural History Museum, and the stone she is cut from glitters with tiny fossils."],"w":"Older than farming, older than writing, older than every king in this puzzle: somebody’s hands made her, and she remains."},{"a":"The Standard of Ur","al":["the standard of ur","standard of ur","the standard"],"c":["A hollow wooden box from a royal tomb at Ur, faced with mosaic figures in lapis lazuli and shell.","One long side marches a column of war chariots and trussed prisoners; the other throws a banquet with a lyre player at the center.","It was buried around 2600 BCE in what is now southern Iraq, alongside a king attended by retainers who died to serve him.","Leonard Woolley dug it from the tomb in the 1920s, in fragments he later fitted back together.","Its war and peace panels now anchor the Mesopotamian galleries of the British Museum."],"w":"Woolley guessed it was a standard carried on a pole, which is where the name comes from, but nobody knows what the box held or why it was buried. The grimmer certainty is the tomb itself: dozens of attendants were arranged to keep serving after death."},{"a":"The Winged Victory of Samothrace","al":["the winged victory","winged victory","the nike of samothrace","victory of samothrace","nike of samothrace"],"c":["A marble Nike, the winged goddess of victory, alighting on the prow of a stone warship.","Her gown clings in a sea wind, and the ship’s base is carved as a churning wave.","She was set up in a sanctuary on Samothrace around 190 BCE, most likely to salute a Rhodian naval triumph.","A French consul dug her from the sanctuary in 1863 and shipped her home in pieces.","She now lands headless at the top of the Louvre’s Daru staircase, wings half spread, the most dramatic staircase in any museum."],"w":"Her arms and head were gone long before excavation; a single upraised hand turned up separately and now lives in a museum in Vienna. The prow beneath her was recovered in later digs, which is why the Louvre display keeps rising off the ground."},{"a":"The Pietà","al":["pieta","the pieta","michelangelo’s pieta","michelangelo's pieta"],"c":["A carving of Mary holding the crucified Christ across her lap, cut from a single block of Carrara marble.","Michelangelo finished it in 1499, still in his twenties, for a French cardinal’s funeral monument.","It is the only work Michelangelo ever signed: he carved his name across the sash after overhearing visitors credit it to another sculptor.","In 1972 a man with a hammer broke off her arm, nose, and eyelid before guards wrestled him away.","It now stands behind bulletproof glass in Saint Peter’s Basilica in Rome, the first masterpiece of the man who would paint the Sistine ceiling."],"w":"The signature across Mary’s sash was added in a fit of pride and later regretted; it remains the only sculpture he ever signed. The 1972 attack cost her an arm, a nose, and an eyelid, all restored during months of quiet repair."},{"a":"The Koh-i-Noor","al":["koh-i-noor","kohinoor","koh-i-noor diamond","the mountain of light"],"c":["A 105.6-carat diamond, now mounted in the crown made for the Queen Mother in 1937.","Its name is Persian for mountain of light, and its documented past reaches back to medieval India.","It passed from Mughal emperors to a Persian shah to a Sikh maharaja, each transfer paid in blood or treachery.","In 1850 it reached Queen Victoria under the Treaty of Lahore, and she wore it as a brooch.","India, Pakistan, Iran, and Afghanistan have all asked for it back, and the argument has outlasted every wearer."],"w":"It was probably mined at Golconda in southern India, and legend says it curses any man who wears it, which conveniently spared the queens. In 1852 Victoria had it recut from 186 carats to its present 105.6, trading nearly half its weight for a brighter sparkle."},{"a":"The Mary Rose","al":["the mary rose","mary rose"],"c":["A Tudor warship that sank in the Solent in 1545 while Henry VIII watched from the shore.","She went down in minutes, leading the English line out against a French invasion fleet.","The fine mud of the seabed sealed her hull, and with it the bones, longbows, dice, shoes, and soup cauldrons of her crew.","She was raised in 1982 before a live worldwide television audience, still cradled in the frame that lifted her.","Her remains now rest in a purpose-built museum at Portsmouth, with bow windows framing the surviving third of her hull."],"w":"Why she sank is still argued: a gust, a sharp turn with the gunports open, or plain overloading all have their advocates. Hundreds of her crew went down with her, and many of their skeletons show the twisted shoulders of professional archers, bent by a lifetime of the longbow."},{"a":"The Spirit of St. Louis","al":["the spirit of st. louis","spirit of st. louis","spirit of saint louis","the spirit of saint louis"],"c":["A single-engine Ryan monoplane named for the hometown of the men who staked the flight.","Because the fuel tank sat directly ahead of the cockpit, Charles Lindbergh steered by periscope and side window.","It was built in about two months in San Diego, fitted with extra tanks holding fuel for a night and a day in the air.","In it, Lindbergh crossed the Atlantic alone in May 1927, New York to Paris in thirty-three and a half hours.","After the flight it never raced again; it went to the Smithsonian, where it hangs beside the Wright Flyer."],"w":"He carried no radio and no parachute, just five sandwiches, two canteens of water, and a wicker chair; weight mattered more than comfort. The plane made one last flight in 1928, from St. Louis to Washington, and has hung in the Smithsonian ever since."},{"a":"The Olmec Heads","al":["the olmec heads","olmec heads","the olmec colossal heads","olmec colossal heads"],"c":["Seventeen colossal basalt heads, some weighing forty tons, carved by Mesoamerica’s first great civilization.","Each wears a rounded helmet with a chin strap and earpieces, gear that scholars link to the ancient ballgame.","They were carved around 1200 to 900 BCE in the lowlands of what is now Veracruz and Tabasco.","The basalt came from the Tuxtlas mountains, and some heads were hauled as far as ninety kilometers with no draft animals and no wheel.","The faces are so individual that most archaeologists read them as portraits of rulers, though no writing survives to name one."],"w":"The Olmec left no readable script, so the heads are the closest thing to a portrait gallery the civilization has. Everything else claimed about its rulers, names included, is inferred from seventeen faces in basalt."},{"a":"The Riace Bronzes","al":["the riace bronzes","riace bronzes","the riace warriors","riace warriors"],"c":["Two over-life-size Greek warriors in bronze, spotted through a diver’s mask off Riace in Calabria in 1972.","The sand had kept them nearly pristine for two thousand years, down to their copper lips and silver teeth.","Each was cast hollow by the lost-wax method, in sections so fine that ancient foundries are still studied through them.","One warrior carries eyes of white stone and the other amber, and both still hold their silver-filled teeth.","They now stand in the museum at Reggio Calabria, generally called the finest original Greek bronzes to survive anywhere."],"w":"Nobody can name the sculptor; the guesses reach from the circle of Phidias on down, and the argument is half a century old. Hauled up from eight meters of sand by a diver on holiday, they are the closest anyone comes to seeing a Greek master’s bronze as it left the foundry."},{"a":"The Benin Bronzes","al":["the benin bronzes","benin bronzes","benin bronze","the benin bronze plaques"],"c":["Thousands of plaques, heads, and figures in brass and bronze once covered the walls of a royal palace in West Africa.","Guilds of master casters made them for the obas of Benin City from perhaps the thirteenth century onward.","Most depict court ceremonies, warriors, and the Portuguese traders who arrived in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries.","A British punitive expedition seized them in 1897, carrying off the palace’s contents as loot.","They are now spread across dozens of museums, and Nigeria has formally asked for them back."],"w":"Most are brass, not true bronze, cast by the lost-wax method that survives in Benin City to this day. The looting of 1897 made them some of the most argued-over objects in the world’s museums, and the argument has not cooled."},{"a":"The Domesday Book","al":["the domesday book","domesday book","domesday","the doomsday book","doomsday book"],"c":["The most complete record of any medieval kingdom, compiled in two volumes by royal scribes.","William the Conqueror ordered the great survey in 1086, twenty years after his invasion of England.","It counts land, ploughs, mills, and people, shire by shire, village by village.","A later cleric compared its rulings to the final judgement, and the nickname stuck for good.","London and Winchester are oddly missing, but the rest of the survey survives complete."],"w":"A chronicler wrote that no single cow, ox, or pig escaped the king’s notice. The book still lives at the National Archives in Kew, nearly a thousand years after the clerks laid down their pens."},{"a":"The Lindisfarne Gospels","al":["the lindisfarne gospels","lindisfarne gospels","the lindisfarne gospel","lindisfarne gospel"],"c":["An illuminated manuscript of the four Gospels, made around the year 700 on a tidal island off Northumberland.","Bishop Eadfrith of Lindisfarne is credited as its scribe and painter, working alone and in layers.","Its ‘carpet pages’ knot together Celtic, Germanic, and Mediterranean patterns in colors ground from distant minerals.","A tenth-century note between its lines translates the Latin into Old English, the earliest surviving Gospel translation into English.","It has belonged to the nation since the 18th century and is kept today in the British Library in London."],"w":"Written in honor of St. Cuthbert, it survived Viking raids, the Dissolution, and centuries of damp. Its painted pages are among the greatest things Britain has ever made, and its blues may have crossed the sea from Afghanistan."},{"a":"The Imperial Fabergé Eggs","al":["the faberge eggs","faberge eggs","the imperial faberge eggs","the faberge imperial eggs","the imperial eggs","the fabergé eggs"],"c":["Jeweled Easter eggs made in St. Petersburg by the House of Fabergé for the last Russian emperors.","The first was an 1885 gift from Tsar Alexander III to his wife, a golden shell hiding a hen, a diamond, and a ruby.","Some fifty imperial eggs were made between 1885 and 1916, and about forty-three have been found.","Each concealed a surprise: a miniature palace, a rosebud, a working model of a Trans-Siberian train.","The revolution ended the series, and the eggs scattered to the Kremlin, to collectors, and to a few still unknown hiding places."],"w":"Peter Carl Fabergé’s workshop charged the tsars fortunes, and today a single egg can outprice a palace. Eight of the imperial eggs have never been located, and the hunt for them never quite cools."},{"a":"The USS Constitution","al":["the uss constitution","uss constitution","old ironsides"],"c":["A three-masted wooden frigate launched in Boston in 1797 and still afloat today.","Her oak hull was so tough that enemy shot seemed to bounce off, earning the nickname Old Ironsides.","In the War of 1812 she defeated HMS Guerriere and HMS Java, and took dozens of merchant ships as well.","She never lost a battle, and her victories helped establish the young American navy’s reputation.","She remains a commissioned warship of the United States Navy, berthed at the Charlestown Navy Yard."],"w":"The oldest commissioned warship still afloat in the world: in 1997, two hundred years after her launch, she sailed Boston Harbor under her own sails one more time, and her crew keeps the rig alive."},{"a":"The Narmer Palette","al":["the narmer palette","narmer palette","the palette of narmer","narmer’s palette"],"c":["A slab of dark siltstone carved on both faces, about 63 centimeters tall, found at Hierakonpolis.","One face shows a king in the white crown smiting a foe; the other shows him in the red crown reviewing captives.","Two long-necked beasts twine their necks at its center, and rows of tiny figures stand beneath.","The king named in its hieroglyphs, Narmer, is often identified with Menes, the unifier of Egypt.","It is displayed in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo, and may be the oldest historical document on Earth."],"w":"Around 3100 BCE, a carving that works like political advertising: the first pharaoh, the two crowns, and the smiting pose that Egyptian kings would repeat for three thousand years."},{"a":"The Stone of Scone","al":["the stone of scone","stone of scone","the stone of destiny","stone of destiny","the coronation stone"],"c":["A block of red sandstone about 51 centimeters long, kept beneath a throne for seven centuries.","Scottish kings were crowned on it at Scone until Edward I of England carried it off in 1296.","It was fitted into a chair at Westminster, and every British monarch since has been crowned above it.","Four Glasgow students stole it back on Christmas Day 1950, and it broke in two in the process.","It went home to Scotland officially in 1996 and now rests in Edinburgh Castle beside the crown jewels."],"w":"Also called the Stone of Destiny. In 2023 it traveled south once more for the coronation of King Charles III, hauled to London in a purpose-built case, as it has been for every crowning since 1308."},{"a":"The Nebra Sky Disc","al":["the nebra sky disc","nebra sky disc","the nebra disc","nebra disc","the sky disc of nebra"],"c":["A bronze disc 30 centimeters across, inlaid with gold sun, moon, and stars, dug from a hilltop in Saxony.","The cluster of seven small gold dots matches the Pleiades as seen three and a half thousand years ago.","It was illegally excavated in 1999 with two swords, and recovered by police in a 2002 sting operation.","At around 1600 BCE it is generally called the oldest known concrete depiction of the cosmos.","It is displayed in the State Museum of Prehistory in Halle, Germany, and in UNESCO’s Memory of the World register."],"w":"The looters’ shovel marks are still part of its story, and so is the layered gold that seems to mark the angle between solstice sunsets. Whoever buried it on the Mittelberg carried a portable sky."},{"a":"The Ishtar Gate","al":["the ishtar gate","ishtar gate","ishtar"],"c":["An enormous gateway of blue glazed brick, raised in Babylon around 575 BCE.","King Nebuchadnezzar II built it as the grand entrance to the city’s processional way.","Rows of golden lions, bulls, and dragons in molded brick honored Ishtar, Adad, and Marduk.","German archaeologists excavated it in the early 1900s and shipped the pieces to Berlin.","Its reconstruction is the centerpiece of the Pergamon Museum."],"w":"A king’s gateway of blue brick, now the pride of a museum an empire never imagined."},{"a":"The Amber Room","al":["the amber room","amber room"],"c":["Wall panels of amber, backed in gold leaf, assembled in Prussia at the start of the 1700s.","Frederick William I shipped the whole room to Peter the Great as a diplomatic gift in 1716.","It glowed for two centuries in the Catherine Palace at Tsarskoye Selo.","Nazi occupiers crated it up in 1941 and carried it off to Königsberg.","It vanished in the chaos of 1945 and has never been found."],"w":"An entire room of amber, gifted, stolen, and lost to the last war."},{"a":"The Scream","al":["the scream","scream","munch’s scream","edvard munch’s the scream"],"c":["Edvard Munch painted the first version in 1893 and revisited the image for two decades.","A figure clutches its skull-like face on a bridge above the Oslofjord at sunset.","The artist wrote that he sensed an infinite scream passing through nature.","One version was stolen from Oslo’s National Gallery in 1994; another from the Munch Museum in 2004.","Both were recovered, and the paintings are back on Norwegian walls."],"w":"The modern world’s most familiar shudder, painted four times over."},{"a":"The Girl with a Pearl Earring","al":["the girl with a pearl earring","girl with a pearl earring","girl with a pearl","vermeer’s girl with a pearl earring"],"c":["Johannes Vermeer painted her around 1665, in oils the size of a large book.","She is not a portrait but a tronie, a study of an imagined face and costume.","No record names the sitter, which has invited centuries of guessing.","The pearl is likely too large to be real; some suspect a drop of glass, others just paint.","She hangs in the Mauritshuis in The Hague, often called the Mona Lisa of the North."],"w":"A stranger with a borrowed pearl who became a Dutch icon."},{"a":"The Night Watch","al":["the night watch","night watch","rembrandt’s night watch"],"c":["Rembrandt finished it in 1642, the same year his wife Saskia died.","Its proper title names a militia company of Amsterdam under Captain Frans Banninck Cocq.","The canvas was trimmed on all sides when it moved to the town hall in 1715.","Dulled varnish made a daylight scene look nocturnal, and so it earned its nickname.","It survived a knife in 1975 and acid in 1990, and now anchors the Rijksmuseum."],"w":"A militia portrait so dramatic that people forget it is one."},{"a":"The Star-Spangled Banner","al":["the star-spangled banner","the star-spangled banner flag","star-spangled banner","the star spangled banner","the fort mchenry flag","the garrison flag"],"c":["Mary Pickersgill and her seamstresses sewed it in Baltimore in the summer of 1813.","It measures about nine by twelve meters, with fifteen stars and fifteen stripes.","It flew over Fort McHenry through the British bombardment of September 1814.","Francis Scott Key watched it still flying at dawn and put the sight into verse.","The Smithsonian has kept it since 1912, now behind glass in a low-oxygen chamber."],"w":"A garrison flag that survived a night of shells and gave a country its anthem."},{"a":"The Tollund Man","al":["the tollund man","tollund man","tollund"],"c":["Peat cutters found him in a Danish bog in 1950, his face so fresh they called the police.","He died in the fourth century BCE, hanged, with the noose still around his neck.","His last meal was a porridge of barley, flax, and wild seeds.","Only his head and one hand are original; the rest was reconstructed from the bog’s evidence.","He rests at the Silkeborg Museum, calm as a sleeping farmer."],"w":"A face from the Iron Age, preserved by a bog that kept its secrets."},{"a":"The Alexander Sarcophagus","al":["the alexander sarcophagus","alexander sarcophagus"],"c":["Workers unearthed it near Sidon in 1887, in a chamber of royal tombs.","Its reliefs show Alexander the Great at the Battle of Issus and on a lion hunt.","It likely held Abdalonymos, a gardener made king of Sidon by Alexander’s favor.","Its carvings still carry traces of the paint that once made the marble blaze.","It has been the prize of the Istanbul Archaeology Museums for over a century."],"w":"A stone coffin that puts Alexander himself in the picture."},{"a":"The Shroud of Turin","al":["the shroud of turin","shroud of turin","the turin shroud","turin shroud","the holy shroud"],"c":["A length of linen about four and a half meters long, bearing the faint image of a bearded man.","It first surfaced in a French village church in the 1350s, already an object of pilgrimage.","The image shows a body with wounds consistent with a crucifixion, front and back.","Radiocarbon tests in 1988 dated the cloth itself to between 1260 and 1390, though the debate never closed.","It has rested in the Cathedral of Turin since 1578, and the church now calls it an icon rather than a relic."],"w":"Whether devotion or medieval craft, it is the most examined textile on Earth, and every generation of science finds something new to argue about."},{"a":"The Little Mermaid","al":["the little mermaid","little mermaid","the mermaid statue","the little mermaid statue"],"c":["A bronze figure just over a meter tall, perched on a rock at the mouth of Copenhagen’s harbor.","Edvard Eriksen sculpted her in 1913, commissioned by the son of the brewer behind Carlsberg.","She was modeled on a ballerina for the face and the sculptor’s wife for the body.","Vandals have beheaded her three times and once knocked her off the rock with explosives.","She sits at Langelinie, unchanged in design, and the city keeps patching her back."],"w":"A statue paid for by beer money, drawn from a Hans Christian Andersen fairy tale, and defended by a city that cannot stop repairing her."},{"a":"The Lascaux Cave","al":["lascaux","lascaux cave","the lascaux cave","the lascaux caves","the lascaux paintings"],"c":["In September 1940, four teenagers and a dog named Robot fell through a hole into a painted world.","The great hall of the bulls runs past aurochs, horses, and deer painted about seventeen thousand years ago.","Over six hundred paintings and fifteen hundred engravings cover its limestone walls.","Breath and footsteps began to destroy it, and the cave was closed to visitors in 1963.","Replicas now take the crowds, while the original stays dark and monitored."],"w":"The best gallery of the Ice Age was found by accident and almost loved to death within two decades."},{"a":"The Parthenon Marbles","al":["the parthenon marbles","parthenon marbles","the elgin marbles","elgin marbles"],"c":["Between 1801 and 1812, agents of Lord Elgin stripped marbles from the Parthenon and shipped them to London.","The haul included about half of the surviving sculptures of the temple built under Pericles.","They went on show in the British Museum in 1817, in a purchase backed by Parliament.","Greece has formally demanded their return since the early 1980s, and the argument has never cooled.","A harsh cleaning in the 1930s scraped away surface detail, which both sides now regret."],"w":"The world’s most famous ownership dispute: one temple’s sculptures split between a museum in Bloomsbury and an Acropolis that waits for them."},{"a":"The Iron Crown of Lombardy","al":["the iron crown of lombardy","iron crown of lombardy","the iron crown","the crown of lombardy"],"c":["A golden circlet set with jewels, its band traditionally said to be hammered from a nail of the True Cross.","It crowned kings of Italy from the early Middle Ages, though its sacred iron has never been verified.","Thirty-three thin plates of gold and enamel hold gemstones in a Byzantine style.","Napoleon took it in Milan in 1805 and crowned himself with it, then declared himself King of Italy.","It rests in the Cathedral of Monza, brought out only for rare exhibitions."],"w":"A crown of legend that crowned real emperors, whose holy iron may be nothing more than a story that stuck."},{"a":"The Quipu","al":["quipu","khipu","the quipu","an inca quipu","inca quipu"],"c":["Knotted cords hanging from a top string, dyed in colors and spun with care.","The Inca used them to run an empire without writing, tracking taxes, census counts, and storehouses.","The knots record numbers in a base-ten positional system that researchers cracked in the 1920s.","Spanish officials burned them by the basketful, so only a few hundred survive.","Post-conquest scribes still read them for a generation, and then the skill was lost."],"w":"The abacus of an empire, and perhaps a writing system that holds narratives no one can read anymore."},{"a":"The Cullinan Diamond","al":["the cullinan diamond","cullinan diamond","the cullinan","the great star of africa","cullinan i"],"c":["At 3,106 carats, it remains the largest gem-quality rough diamond ever found.","Fred Wells saw it glitter in the wall of a South African mine in January 1905 and first took it for crystal.","It was named for the mine’s owner and presented to King Edward VII on his birthday.","It cut into nine major stones and ninety-six brilliants, with the two biggest joining the Crown Jewels.","Cullinan I, the Great Star of Africa, rides in the Sovereign’s Sceptre at every coronation."],"w":"A stone so large it was cut in Amsterdam in secret and sent to London by ordinary registered post while a decoy crossed by sea."},{"a":"The Hoxne Hoard","al":["the hoxne hoard","hoxne hoard","the hoxne treasure"],"c":["In 1992, a Suffolk farmer asked a friend with a metal detector to find his lost hammer.","Instead, the machine found a Roman hoard of nearly fifteen thousand coins.","Silver tableware, gold jewelry, and pepper shakers came up with them, buried after 407 CE.","The finders split a reward that paid off a mortgage and more.","The hoard, the hammer, and the detector are all now in the British Museum."],"w":"The largest cache of late Roman gold and silver found in Britain, dug up while looking for a hand tool."},{"a":"The Birth of Venus","al":["the birth of venus","birth of venus","botticelli’s birth of venus","the botticelli venus"],"c":["Sandro Botticelli painted it around 1485 for the Medici circle in Florence.","Venus stands on a shell, blown ashore by winds and met by a maid with a cloak.","Its naked goddess was startling for its day, painted for a private villa rather than a church.","Tempera on canvas, a fragile choice that has kept the colors pale and luminous.","It has hung in the Uffizi since the 1800s, the emblem of Renaissance beauty itself."],"w":"The best-loved face of the Florentine Renaissance, painted for one family’s room and now claimed by everyone."},{"a":"The Diamond Sutra","al":["the diamond sutra","diamond sutra"],"c":["A seventeen-foot paper scroll, printed in China in 868 and dated in its own colophon.","It is the world’s oldest surviving dated printed book, six centuries older than Gutenberg.","Aurel Stein carried it away from the sealed library cave at Dunhuang in 1907.","The cave held tens of thousands of manuscripts, stacked and walled up around the year 1000.","Its frontispiece shows the Buddha teaching, and the scroll is now held in the British Library."],"w":"Proof that printing was ancient before Europe tried it: a Buddhist scroll that still opens cleanly after eleven centuries."},{"a":"The Voynich Manuscript","al":["the voynich manuscript","voynich manuscript","voynich"],"c":["A book of about 240 vellum pages written in a script no one has ever read.","Its pages circle unknown plants, naked figures in green pools, and wheels of stars.","Carbon dating puts its parchment in the early fifteenth century, likely Italy or Central Europe.","It passed through the hands of alchemists and emperors before Wilfrid Voynich bought it in 1912.","Every generation of codebreakers, including the Bletchley veterans, has attacked it and retired undefeated."],"w":"Six centuries of failed decipherment: the book reads to everyone and no one, and Yale’s Beinecke Library keeps it under the shelfmark MS 408."},{"a":"The Hereford Mappa Mundi","al":["the hereford mappa mundi","hereford mappa mundi","mappa mundi","the hereford map"],"c":["The largest surviving medieval map of the world drawn on a single sheet of vellum.","Jerusalem sits at its exact center, and east rather than north is at the top.","Real cities and rivers share space with the monstrous races said to live at the world’s edge.","It was drawn around 1300, likely in the workshop of Richard of Holdingham.","It hangs in Hereford Cathedral, chained where pilgrims could read creation at a glance."],"w":"A whole medieval worldview in one calfskin: Christ above, Jerusalem in the middle, and England pushed to the very bottom of the frame."},{"a":"The Ardabil Carpet","al":["the ardabil carpet","ardabil carpet","ardabil"],"c":["One of the oldest dated Persian carpets in the world, and among the largest.","It was woven in 1539 from silk and wool, with a signature couplet worked into one end.","Its sunburst medallion within a field of arabesques set the template for Persian carpets for centuries.","It is one of a matched pair; the second carpet was cut up in the Victorian era to fill gaps.","The larger, whole carpet hangs in the Victoria and Albert Museum in London."],"w":"Maqsud of Kashan signed the masterwork in 1539; its cut up twin was patched back together and crossed the Atlantic to Los Angeles."},{"a":"The Piri Reis Map","al":["the piri reis map","piri reis map","piri reis"],"c":["A fragment of a world map drawn on gazelle skin in 1513 for an Ottoman admiral.","Its cartographer credits some twenty source maps, including some, he says, from the age of Alexander.","It shows the coasts of Europe, Africa, and a strangely bulging South America with surprising accuracy.","It was rediscovered in 1929 in the Topkapi Palace archives and caused an instant sensation.","Its author was executed in 1554, and the rest of the map has never surfaced."],"w":"Piri Reis drew it in 1513, a decade after Columbus; the fragment is now in Istanbul’s Topkapi Palace, and its missing two thirds have never been found."},{"a":"The Codex Gigas","al":["the codex gigas","codex gigas","the devil’s bible"],"c":["The largest surviving medieval manuscript in the world, thirty six kilograms of vellum in a wooden binding.","Legend says a monk sentenced to be walled up alive wrote it in a single night with the devil’s help.","It holds the complete Bible plus encyclopedia, chronicles, and medical texts, all in one remarkably even hand.","It is famous for its full page portrait of the Devil, drawn grinning and alone.","It was looted from Prague by the Swedish army in 1648 and has been in Stockholm ever since."],"w":"One scribe, one astonishing hand, one famous devil: the largest medieval manuscript in the world is Sweden’s biggest book and Bohemia’s biggest loss."},{"a":"The Ghent Altarpiece","al":["the ghent altarpiece","ghent altarpiece","the adoration of the mystic lamb"],"c":["A hinged polyptych of twenty panels painted for a church in Flanders and finished in 1432.","The van Eyck brothers painted it, and oils made its colors glow as tempera never could.","Its central scene shows a lamb on an altar, worshipped by saints, martyrs, and hermits.","It has been stolen or taken apart more times than almost any artwork in history.","One panel, the Just Judges, has been missing since it was stolen in 1934 and never recovered."],"w":"Hubert and Jan van Eyck’s masterpiece has survived iconoclasts, Napoleon, and the Nazis; the Just Judges panel stolen in 1934 was never found and hangs there as a copy."},{"a":"The Dresden Codex","al":["the dresden codex","dresden codex"],"c":["One of only four surviving books of the pre-Columbian Maya.","Its folded bark pages track the calendar of Venus and tables of eclipses centuries deep.","It was already old when Europeans arrived, likely painted in the fourteenth century or earlier.","How it reached Europe is unclear; it surfaced in the royal library of Saxony by 1739.","Water used to fight the bombing of Dresden in 1945 burned and blurred several of its pages."],"w":"A Maya astronomical almanac that survived twice: first the burning of the books, then the fire bombing of the city that kept it."},{"a":"The Oseberg Ship","al":["the oseberg ship","oseberg ship","oseberg"],"c":["A Viking ship buried in a mound of blue clay in Norway around the year 834.","Two women, one elderly and one younger, were laid in it with beds, sledges, and tapestries.","The clay sealed it so well that its carved oak survived eleven centuries nearly intact.","Its stem and stern rise in a graceful spiral carved with gripping beast ornament.","It was dug out of the mound in 1904 and now rests in the museum at Bygdøy in Oslo."],"w":"A burial ship sealed in blue clay: the carving survived eleven centuries, the identities of the two women did not, and the hull is now too fragile to sail."},{"a":"The Euphronios Krater","al":["the euphronios krater","euphronios krater","euphronios"],"c":["A wine mixing bowl painted in Athens around 515 BCE, signed by its potter and its painter.","Its scenes show the death of Sarpedon, a son of Zeus, carried off the field by Sleep and Death.","It is the only complete work of the painter Euphronios to survive from antiquity.","It left Italy in 1972 in circumstances its acquiring museum never explained, for a record price.","After decades of argument it went home to Italy, and the Met kept some disputed pots on loan."],"w":"Euphronios’ only complete survivor: dug up in Etruria, smuggled out in 1972, and returned to Italy in 2008, where it anchors the Villa Giulia’s collection."},{"a":"The Garden of Earthly Delights","al":["the garden of earthly delights","garden of earthly delights","the bosch garden"],"c":["A triptych painted around 1500, opening onto paradise on the left and hell on the right.","Its center panel teems with nude figures riding beasts, eating giant fruit, and floating in pools.","Birds, berries, and musical instruments appear at impossible scale, and nothing explains itself.","The painter spent his life in a quiet Dutch town and left no writings about what it means.","It was owned by a Spanish king by 1591 and now hangs in the Prado in Madrid."],"w":"Bosch’s painted riddle: Eden, orgy, and nightmare in three panels, five centuries of viewers, and still no agreement on what the joke is."},{"a":"The Gundestrup Cauldron","al":["gundestrup cauldron","the gundestrup cauldron","gundestrup silver cauldron"],"c":["In 1891 peat cutters in a Jutland bog struck a pile of hammered silver plates, stacked one inside another.","Reassembled it stands 42 centimeters tall and weighs just under nine kilograms, the largest silverwork to survive from Iron Age Europe.","One panel shows an antlered god gripping a horned serpent, and another a god holding a wheel; the scenes have kept scholars arguing ever since.","The silverwork technique reads Thracian and the imagery Celtic, and neither side of that debate has won yet.","It is displayed at the National Museum of Denmark in Copenhagen."],"w":"A bog kept the question safe: the Iron Age’s greatest silver riddle, dug out by a peat spade."},{"a":"The Nag Hammadi Codices","al":["nag hammadi codices","the nag hammadi codices","nag hammadi library","the nag hammadi library"],"c":["In 1945 a farmer digging for fertilizer near the cliffs of Upper Egypt pulled a sealed jar of old books out of the ground.","Thirteen codices of papyrus in leather covers, copied in Coptic around the fourth century.","One holds a Gospel of Thomas: 114 sayings of Jesus, with no cross and no empty tomb.","They are the gnostic scriptures the canon left out, hidden by hands unknown and safe in their jar.","Most of the find is kept today in the Coptic Museum in Cairo."],"w":"A sealed jar outlasted the verdict of the young church: the heresies themselves, found sixteen centuries after the burying."},{"a":"The Lycurgus Cup","al":["lycurgus cup","the lycurgus cup","lycurgus"],"c":["A Roman cage cup of glass, no bigger than a fist, carved with a figure tangled in vines.","Made in the fourth century CE, it shows the death of the king Lycurgus at the hands of the vine.","Green when the light strikes it, it glows ruby red when a lamp is lit inside it.","The color comes from gold and silver particles far too small to see, dissolved in the glass itself.","It stands in the British Museum, unmatched by anything else the Roman world left."],"w":"Roman glassworkers studded this cup with particles of gold a thousand years before the microscope existed: it glows red from within, and no one could say why until the twentieth century."},{"a":"The Younger Memnon","al":["younger memnon","the younger memnon","memnon","ramesses colossus"],"c":["A seven-ton granite head and shoulders, all of a pharaoh that a single ship could carry.","Ramesses II had it carved for his funeral temple at Thebes, one of a pair that flanked the gate.","The Greeks gave the statue a hero’s name, mistaking the pharaoh for their own Memnon.","The showman Giovanni Belzoni dragged it to the Nile in 1816, and it reached London in 1821.","News of the journey inspired a sonnet about a shattered colossus whose boast is still quoted."],"w":"Shelley wrote Ozymandias after hearing this broken giant was coming to London; the poem outlived the empire it mourned and made Ramesses immortal twice."},{"a":"The Seated Scribe","al":["seated scribe","the seated scribe","scribe of saqqara","the scribe of saqqara"],"c":["A painted wooden figure of a clerk, cross-legged with a papyrus unrolled on his lap.","His eyes are inlaid crystal set in copper, and they still seem to follow you across the room.","He was dug from the sands of Saqqara in 1850, from the age of the pyramid builders.","Middle-aged and soft-bellied, he is startlingly real next to the serene statues of kings.","He has sat in the Louvre for over a century, brush in hand, waiting to take dictation."],"w":"One sculptor in the Old Kingdom made a clerk immortal: realistic, tired, and alert, everything royal art was not supposed to be."},{"a":"The Book of the Dead of Hunefer","al":["book of the dead of hunefer","the book of the dead of hunefer","hunefer","hunefer book of the dead"],"c":["A long painted papyrus rolled up beside a dead man, spells for the trials ahead.","Its owner was a royal scribe and overseer of cattle, a grand man of Thebes.","Its most famous scene weighs his heart against a feather while a monster waits by the scales.","British collectors carried it off in the nineteenth century, and the British Museum has kept it since.","Nearly every textbook picture of the Egyptian afterlife comes from this one scroll."],"w":"The weighing of the heart, the image everyone knows, survives because one family bought their scribe a first-class copy of the afterlife."},{"a":"The Behistun Inscription","al":["behistun inscription","the behistun inscription","behistun","bisitun inscription"],"c":["A cliff face in the Zagros mountains, cut with the same boast in three kinds of cuneiform.","Darius the Great had it carved high above the road to record his victories over nine rival kings.","Below the king stand his prisoners, roped together by the neck from his bow.","A British officer copied the text in the 1830s and 1840s, working from ropes and ladders below it.","It unlocked cuneiform exactly as one gray slab in London had unlocked hieroglyphs."],"w":"Darius hung his press release forty meters up a cliff where nobody could edit it; Henry Rawlinson dangled below it on ropes, and Persia’s dead language began to speak."},{"a":"The Deluge Tablet","al":["deluge tablet","the deluge tablet","the flood tablet","flood tablet","gilgamesh flood tablet"],"c":["A single clay tablet from the buried library of a king of Nineveh.","It tells how the gods warned one man to tear down his house and build a boat.","Its flood, its grounded ark, and its birds all come a thousand years before another famous version.","An assistant in the British Museum read it aloud in 1872, and the story goes he grew so excited he began undressing in the reading room.","It is the eleventh tablet of the Epic of Gilgamesh."],"w":"The flood story the Bible would inherit, dug from Nineveh and read aloud in 1872 to a room that realized Noah had an older brother."},{"a":"The Mask of Warka","al":["mask of warka","the mask of warka","the lady of warka","lady of warka","sumerian mask of warka"],"c":["A thin face of white marble with hollow eyes and joined brows, five thousand years old.","It comes from Uruk, the world’s first city, from the temple quarter of its goddess.","It may be the first life-size human face anyone ever carved.","Looters carried it off when the Iraq Museum fell in 2003; a tip brought it back from a crate buried on a farm.","Back in Baghdad, it is nicknamed the Mona Lisa of Mesopotamia."],"w":"The first city’s first portrait, looted in a week of chaos and dug out of a field by an informant’s tip: five thousand years old and nearly lost twice."},{"a":"The Timbuktu Manuscripts","al":["timbuktu manuscripts","the timbuktu manuscripts","manuscripts of timbuktu","timbuktu manuscript collection"],"c":["Leather-bound books by the hundreds of thousands, gathered at the edge of the Sahara.","They hold medieval mathematics, astronomy, law and poetry, much of it still uncatalogued.","Their city of salt and gold was a byword for the middle of nowhere, and also a university town.","In 2012, as war closed on northern Mali, librarians began smuggling them south in crates and boats.","Bundled in fruit boxes and couriers’ bags, most of them reached safety around Bamako."],"w":"A city famous for being nowhere nearly lost its memory to a war; families and librarians carried it out by the crate, one book at a time."},{"a":"The Archimedes Palimpsest","al":["archimedes palimpsest","the archimedes palimpsest","archimedes manuscript","the archimedes manuscript"],"c":["A small book of prayers written in 1229 over ink scraped away a thousand years before.","Underneath the prayers sit Greek treatises, including works of Archimedes lost everywhere else.","One of them, the Method, shows him juggling infinities like a modern mathematician.","A Danish scholar spotted it in Constantinople in 1906; later hands painted forgeries over some pages.","X-rays and ultraviolet light in the 2000s read the scraped text almost whole."],"w":"A monk washed Archimedes off his pages to make a prayer book; eight centuries later, particle physics washed the prayers back off."},{"a":"The Herculaneum Papyri","al":["herculaneum papyri","the herculaneum papyri","herculaneum scrolls","the herculaneum scrolls"],"c":["A whole library turned to charcoal by the same eruption that buried Pompeii.","The scrolls came out of a villa’s cellar at Herculaneum in the 1750s, black as logs.","Attempts to unroll them tore most of the library apart.","What survives is largely Greek philosophy, above all the works of the Epicureans.","They are now being read without ever being opened, by X-ray beams and learning machines."],"w":"The only library to survive from antiquity survived as charcoal; machine learning is now reading scrolls no human hand dares unroll."},{"a":"The Vindolanda Tablets","al":["vindolanda tablets","the vindolanda tablets","vindolanda letters","the vindolanda letters"],"c":["Wafer-thin slivers of wood, the memo paper of a Roman fort in Britain.","They survived because the mud they fell into never let the air in.","One is a birthday invitation between two officers’ wives, among the earliest Latin known from a woman’s hand.","Others list soldiers’ rations: beer, socks, and linen underpants for the frontier winter.","Written in ink around 100 CE, they are the oldest surviving letters from Britain."],"w":"Roman Britain wrote home on birchwood: birthday parties, beer rations, and woolly underwear, kept fifteen hundred years by airless mud."},{"a":"The Tabula Peutingeriana","al":["tabula peutingeriana","the tabula peutingeriana","peutinger map","the peutinger map","peutinger table"],"c":["A single parchment strip, meters long, showing the Roman world from Britain to India.","Roads thread between hundreds of towns, with tiny figures marking inns, baths and temples.","Rome sits enthroned at its center, the hub of everything.","It is a medieval copy of a Roman original, named for the sixteenth-century collector who owned it.","It lives in Vienna’s national library, too fragile now to be shown often."],"w":"The Roman empire as a courier saw it: one long ribbon of roads and rest stops, the only such itinerary to survive, if only as a copy."},{"a":"The Lion Capital of Ashoka","al":["lion capital of ashoka","the lion capital of ashoka","ashoka capital","the lion capital","sarnath lion capital"],"c":["A pillar top of polished sandstone: four lions standing back to back, gazing outward.","It was raised around 250 BCE at the deer park where the Buddha first taught.","The emperor who set it up had made one war too many and spent the rest of his reign renouncing them.","The wheel beneath the lions now sits at the center of India’s flag.","Excavated at Sarnath in the 1900s, it became the emblem of the republic of India."],"w":"India’s state emblem is a sermon in stone: an emperor’s vow of peace, carved at the Buddha’s own deer park and adopted two thousand years later."},{"a":"The Iron Pillar of Delhi","al":["iron pillar of delhi","the iron pillar of delhi","delhi iron pillar","the iron pillar"],"c":["A column of nearly pure wrought iron, taller than two men, standing beside a Delhi mosque.","It was raised in the fourth century CE to honor a king the inscription calls Chandra.","Sixteen centuries of monsoon have left it almost untouched by rust.","The metal’s surface grows its own protective skin, a trick metallurgists still argue over.","Pilgrims long tried to encircle it with their arms behind their backs for luck."],"w":"A seventeen-hundred-year-old column of iron that refuses to rust, standing in the Indian capital as the world’s most stubborn metallurgy puzzle."},{"a":"The St Cuthbert Gospel","al":["st cuthbert gospel","the st cuthbert gospel","st cuthbert’s gospel","the st cuthbert’s gospel","stonyhurst gospel"],"c":["A gospel book hardly bigger than a hand, bound in red goatskin tooled with a border.","It was made in northeast England near the year 698 and laid in a saint’s coffin.","Monks carried that coffin around for over two centuries, fleeing Viking raids.","When it was opened at Durham in 1104, the little book inside was still perfect.","The British Library bought it in 2012 with a record appeal; it is Europe’s oldest book with its original binding."],"w":"Europe’s oldest intact book spent four centuries lying with a dead saint and came out unread and unspoiled, the medieval north’s own time capsule."},{"a":"The Unicorn Tapestries","al":["unicorn tapestries","the unicorn tapestries","the hunt of the unicorn","hunt of the unicorn","the unicorn tapestry"],"c":["Seven great wall hangings of wool and silk, woven in the Netherlands around 1500.","They follow a unicorn hunt from the chase to its end, alive in a garden under a pomegranate tree.","Readers of the allegory see Christ, or a wedding, or a hunt that never needed explaining at the time.","The initials A and a reversed E, worked into the sky, name an owner nobody has identified.","They hang today in a museum built like a monastery at the top of Manhattan."],"w":"The finest medieval tapestries anywhere tell a hunt no one has decoded for five hundred years, signed with the initials of a lost patron."},{"a":"The Franks Casket","al":["franks casket","the franks casket","auzon casket","the auzon casket"],"c":["A little box of whalebone, carved over every face from lid to base.","Its panels mix the Magi at the manger with the smith Weland and the she-wolf of Rome.","Runes ring the rim, posing a riddle about the sea that gave up the bone.","It was sawn apart by a French family and sold piece by piece in the nineteenth century.","One lost panel turned up in Italy and rejoined the rest in the British Museum."],"w":"Whalebone carved by a Northumbrian poet-smith: scripture, legend and Rome on a few inches of bone, and a rune riddle about the whale itself."},{"a":"The Alfred Jewel","al":["alfred jewel","the alfred jewel"],"c":["A pointed frame of gold holding a clear crystal over a tiny enameled figure.","Around the rim, Old English reads: Alfred ordered me made.","It is the one certain relic of the ninth-century king who fought the Vikings and saved English learning.","A laborer dug it out of a Somerset field in 1693.","It probably crowned the pointer rod of one of the gift sets Alfred sent his bishops with translated books."],"w":"The one object tied to Alfred’s own reign by its own words: the ornate head of a reading pointer, inscribed in English, found by a ditchdigger after eight centuries."},{"a":"The Holy Crown of Hungary","al":["holy crown of hungary","the holy crown of hungary","crown of saint stephen","st stephen’s crown","the crown of st stephen"],"c":["A crown of gold bands and enamel portraits, holy to a whole kingdom for centuries.","Its top cross was bent long ago, and stays bent; nobody dares straighten it.","Hungarian law held that the crown itself, not the man who wore it, was the true sovereign.","Its keepers handed it to the American army at the end of the Second World War, and it spent decades at Fort Knox.","It came home in 1978 and now stands under guard in the parliament in Budapest."],"w":"A bent cross and two fused bands: the crown Hungarians once judged the country’s true ruler, exiled to Kentucky and returned by Jimmy Carter."},{"a":"The Battersea Shield","al":["battersea shield","the battersea shield"],"c":["A bronze shield-face covered in swirling patterns of red enamel.","Dredgers pulled it from the Thames near Chelsea in the 1850s.","Its triple spirals are the La Tène style, the classic image of Iron Age Celtic art.","A thin bronze skin with no wooden back, it was surely a gift to the river and never meant for battle.","It has hung in the British Museum ever since, the face of ancient Britain’s art."],"w":"Too fine for war and sunk in the Thames on purpose: the river gave back the greatest piece of Celtic art ever found in Britain."},{"a":"The Holy Lance","al":["holy lance","the holy lance","lance of longinus","spear of destiny"],"c":["Kept today in the imperial treasury of the Hofburg in Vienna.","Legend says it is the spear that pierced the side of Christ, named for the soldier Longinus.","A rough iron nail is fastened to its blade with bands of silver, said to be one of the nails of the Crucifixion.","Emperor Sigismund lodged it with the city of Nuremberg in 1424, and it stayed there for centuries.","American troops found it in a bank vault in 1945 and returned it to Vienna a year later."],"w":"A spear blade carrying a nail of the Cross, carried through a thousand years of imperial ceremony and twentieth-century war."},{"a":"The Crown of Thorns","al":["crown of thorns","the crown of thorns","holy crown of thorns"],"c":["Venerated as the woven ring of thorns placed on Christ before the crucifixion.","Louis IX of France brought it from Constantinople in 1238 and raised the Sainte-Chapelle to house it.","It survived the Revolution and has been kept at Notre-Dame in Paris for over two centuries.","Firefighters carried it out of the burning cathedral on the night of April 15, 2019.","It is shown to the faithful on set days each year, kept now in a ring of crystal."],"w":"The relic behind a royal chapel of glass and a modern rescue watched by the whole world."},{"a":"The Trundholm Sun Chariot","al":["trundholm sun chariot","the trundholm sun chariot","trundholm chariot","sun chariot"],"c":["A bronze horse on wheels draws a great disk, as if hauling the sun across the sky.","A farmer turned it up from a bog on the Danish island of Zealand in 1902.","Made in the Nordic Bronze Age, about three and a half thousand years ago.","The disk is sheeted in gold on one face only, usually read as day and night.","It stands in the National Museum of Denmark in Copenhagen, the face of Bronze Age religion."],"w":"The oldest surviving picture of an idea that moved the ancient world: the sun as a daily journey."},{"a":"The Sword of Goujian","al":["sword of goujian","the sword of goujian","goujian sword","goujian’s sword"],"c":["A bronze sword from the Spring and Autumn period, about two and a half thousand years old.","Digging in 1965 in Hubei province uncovered it in a flooded tomb complex.","Its blade came out untarnished and still sharp after more than two millennia underground.","An inscription of eight characters names its owner: Goujian, king of Yue.","Rhombus patterns and blue inlay still cover the blade, which sits in the Hubei Provincial Museum."],"w":"A king’s blade that cheated time itself, and the standing puzzle of why the bronze never tarnished."},{"a":"The Lyre of Ur","al":["lyre of ur","the lyre of ur","bull headed lyre","great lyre of ur","queen’s lyre"],"c":["One of the world’s oldest stringed instruments, about four and a half thousand years old.","Leonard Woolley’s excavations of the Royal Cemetery of Ur brought it to light in the 1920s.","Its sound box bears a bull’s head of gold and lapis lazuli, with a beard of the same blue stone.","Several lyres lay in the royal tombs, the wood of their bodies long since rotted away.","Reconstructed examples stand in the British Museum and the Penn Museum in Philadelphia."],"w":"Music with a face: the oldest lyres on Earth, pulled from the death pits of Sumer’s kings."},{"a":"The Warren Cup","al":["warren cup","the warren cup"],"c":["A Roman silver drinking cup of the first century CE.","Two relief scenes wrap its sides, both of male lovers in bed.","Customs officers refused it entry to the United States in the 1950s as obscene matter.","It stayed in private cupboards for most of the twentieth century.","The British Museum bought it in 1999, and it is now one of its most-studied Roman pieces."],"w":"A frank silver window on Roman desire, hidden for nineteen centuries and honoured at last."},{"a":"The Berlin Gold Hat","al":["berlin gold hat","the berlin gold hat","gold hat","golden hat of berlin"],"c":["A tall cone of thin beaten gold, worked in bands of rings, suns and crescents.","Made in the European Bronze Age, more than three thousand years ago.","Only four such golden hats are known, from France and southern Germany.","Berlin acquired this one from the art market in 1996; its findspot was never recorded.","Some scholars read its bands as a lunisolar calendar, a Bronze Age almanac in gold."],"w":"A priest’s crown from a world without writing, possibly carrying a calendar nobody can prove."},{"a":"The Merneptah Stele","al":["merneptah stele","the merneptah stele","israel stele","victory stele of merneptah"],"c":["A black granite slab carved for Pharaoh Merneptah around 1208 BCE.","Flinders Petrie dug it from the pharaoh’s funerary temple at Thebes in 1896.","Its hymn of victory lists the lands crushed in a Libyan war.","Among them stands the only Egyptian mention of a people called Israel.","It holds the earliest known reference to Israel outside the Bible, and sits in Cairo’s museum."],"w":"One boastful line in a pharaoh’s victory song, and the first footstep of Israel in the record."},{"a":"The Mesha Stele","al":["mesha stele","the mesha stele","moabite stone","moab stone"],"c":["A black basalt stone raised by Mesha, king of Moab, in the ninth century BCE.","Its Moabite inscription boasts of his revolt against the kingdom of Israel.","Found in 1868 at Dhiban in Jordan, it broke apart in a dispute over its sale.","Squeezes taken before the break let scholars reassemble most of the text.","It has stood in the Louvre since 1873, Moab’s own account of a war the Bible tells otherwise."],"w":"The other side of an Israelite war, told in stone by the enemy king who won it."},{"a":"The Tel Dan Stele","al":["tel dan stele","the tel dan stele","house of david inscription","tel dan inscription"],"c":["Fragments of a basalt slab unearthed at Tel Dan in northern Israel in 1993 and 1994.","The surviving text is Aramaic, cut in the ninth century BCE.","It was set up by a king of Damascus boasting of victories over Israel and its neighbour.","The broken lines carry the earliest known reference to the House of David outside the Bible.","The pieces are kept in the Israel Museum in Jerusalem."],"w":"Two surviving words, House of David, and the first outside witness to Israel’s greatest king."},{"a":"The Charioteer of Delphi","al":["charioteer of delphi","the charioteer of delphi","delphi charioteer"],"c":["A life-size bronze of a young charioteer, cast around 470 BCE.","It honoured a chariot-racing victory won by Polyzalos, ruler of Gela in Sicily.","His reins still wrap the standing figure, whose lashes are inlaid in copper.","An earthquake that wrecked the sanctuary in 373 BCE buried him, and so saved him.","He stands in the museum at Delphi, the calmest survivor of Greek racing."],"w":"The driver at the moment of victory: early classical calm in bronze, saved by the quake that buried him."},{"a":"The Augustus of Prima Porta","al":["augustus of prima porta","the augustus of prima porta","prima porta augustus"],"c":["A marble statue of Rome’s first emperor, just over two metres tall.","Diggers found it in 1863 at Prima Porta, in the ruins of his wife Livia’s villa.","It is a marble copy of a bronze original that celebrated the return of the standards from Parthia.","The breastplate shows that diplomatic triumph, with gods and provinces gathered round it.","A cupid on a dolphin rides at his leg, claiming descent from Venus; it hangs in the Vatican."],"w":"Imperial Rome’s chosen image of itself: the heir of Venus, in the pose of a Greek athlete."},{"a":"The Boxer at Rest","al":["boxer at rest","the boxer at rest","seated boxer","the seated boxer","boxer of the quirinal"],"c":["A Hellenistic bronze of a seated boxer, sculpted in the third or second century BCE.","Workmen found him in 1885, buried on the Quirinal Hill in Rome.","His nose is broken and his ears are the flattened ears of a fighter.","Wounds and drops of blood are inlaid in copper across his face and arms.","He still wears the leather hand-wraps of the ancient ring, and sits in Rome’s Palazzo Massimo."],"w":"Not a victor but a veteran: Greek sculpture’s greatest portrait of a working body in pain."},{"a":"The Artemesion Bronze","al":["artemision bronze","the artemision bronze","poseidon of artemision","zeus of artemision"],"c":["An over-life-size bronze god, cast around 460 BCE and armed for a throw.","Sponge divers found him in the sea off Cape Artemision in Euboea in the 1920s.","The same wreck yielded a bronze horse and its tiny jockey.","His weapon is gone, so scholars still argue: Zeus with a thunderbolt, or Poseidon with a trident.","He strides today in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens."],"w":"A god caught mid-throw from the seafloor, and a standing argument over which god he is."},{"a":"The Derveni Krater","al":["derveni krater","the derveni krater","derveni crater"],"c":["A great bronze wine-mixing vessel sheathed in reliefs of gold leaf.","Its frieze runs with Dionysos, dancing maenads and satyrs.","Builders found it in 1962 in a rich tomb near Thessaloniki.","Made in the fourth century BCE, it served at a feast and then held a Macedonian’s ashes.","It stands in the museum at Thessaloniki, the richest bronze ever buried in Greece."],"w":"A Greek party vessel sent into a Macedonian tomb, gold on bronze for one last symposium."},{"a":"The Mildenhall Treasure","al":["mildenhall treasure","the mildenhall treasure","mildenhall hoard","mildenhall silver"],"c":["Thirty-four pieces of Roman silver tableware from the fourth century CE.","A ploughman turned them up near Mildenhall in Suffolk in 1942.","The finder kept the hoard at home for four years before an inquest declared it treasure.","Its Great Dish, nearly sixty centimetres wide, carries a drunken rout of Bacchus amid sea gods.","The whole hoard went to the British Museum, the finest Roman silver found in Britain."],"w":"A Roman dinner service buried in a Suffolk field, with the empire’s whole mythology on one dish."},{"a":"The Shrine of the Three Kings","al":["shrine of the three kings","the shrine of the three kings","shrine of the magi","reliquary of the three kings"],"c":["A gold reliquary in Cologne Cathedral, begun in the 1190s by Nicholas of Verdun.","It is said to hold the bones of the Three Magi, taken from Milan in 1164 by Barbarossa’s forces.","The largest reliquary in the Western world, shaped like a basilica in gold and silver.","More than a thousand gemstones, pearls and antique cameos stud its walls.","Its reliefs run from the prophets to the life of Christ, a bible in metal."],"w":"The tomb of the gift-bringers of Christmas, in a chest of gold that drew a cathedral up around it."},{"a":"The Regent Diamond","al":["regent diamond","the regent diamond","pitt diamond","the pitt diamond"],"c":["A cushion-cut diamond of about 141 carats, born from a rough stone of 410.","Thomas Pitt, governor of Madras, bought it in India and had it cut in London over two years.","In 1717 it passed to the Duke of Orleans, regent of France, and took his name.","It glittered on the coronation crown of Louis XV and in the hilt of Napoleon’s sword.","It survived the Revolution intact and shines in the Galerie d’Apollon of the Louvre."],"w":"A diamond that rode the whole arc of French royalty, from an Indian mine to a glass case in Paris."},{"a":"The Bell X-1","al":["bell x-1","the bell x-1","glamorous glennis"],"c":["A rocket-powered research plane shaped like a fifty-calibre bullet.","On October 14, 1947, Chuck Yeager flew it faster than sound above the California desert.","The first piloted supersonic flight in history.","Yeager named it Glamorous Glennis, after his wife.","Launched from a bomber rather than a runway, it now hangs in the Smithsonian in Washington."],"w":"The orange bullet in which the sound barrier fell, ridden by a pilot flying on broken ribs."},{"a":"The Friendship 7 Capsule","al":["friendship 7","friendship seven","the friendship 7 capsule"],"c":["The Mercury spacecraft John Glenn rode on February 20, 1962.","It carried the first American flight to orbit the Earth, three times around.","Glenn chose the name Friendship 7 himself.","A faulty signal made mission control fear the heat shield was loose, and Glenn rode home with the retropack still on.","Fished from the Atlantic after splashdown, it now belongs to the Smithsonian."],"w":"The can in which an American first circled the planet, and a test pilot trusted the engineers home."},{"a":"The Jules Rimet Trophy","al":["jules rimet trophy","the jules rimet trophy","jules rimet cup"],"c":["The original World Cup trophy: a gilt statuette of Nike, goddess of victory.","Named for Jules Rimet, the FIFA president who founded the tournament in 1930.","An Italian official hid it through the war in a shoebox under his bed.","Stolen in London in 1966, it was found under a hedge by a dog named Pickles.","Given outright to Brazil in 1970, it was stolen again in 1983 and probably melted down."],"w":"Football’s first holy grail, twice stolen, once found by a dog, and finally lost to a furnace."},{"a":"The Ruby Slippers","al":["ruby slippers","the ruby slippers","wizard of oz shoes","dorothy’s shoes"],"c":["The red shoes Judy Garland wore down the yellow brick road in the 1939 film.","In L. Frank Baum’s book they were silver; the film made them ruby to blaze in Technicolor.","Satin pumps covered in red sequins, made in several pairs for the shoot.","One pair was stolen from the Judy Garland Museum in 2005 and recovered by the FBI in 2018.","Another pair sold at auction in 2024 for about twenty-eight million dollars."],"w":"Cinema’s most famous footwear: silver shoes turned ruby for color film, and now among its priciest relics."},{"a":"The Kon-Tiki","al":["kon-tiki","the kon-tiki","kon tiki"],"c":["A raft of nine balsa logs, named for a pre-Inca sun god.","Thor Heyerdahl and five crew sailed it from Peru into the Pacific in 1947.","One hundred and one days and nearly seven thousand kilometres carried it to Raroia, an atoll in the Tuamotus.","The voyage argued, against the scholars, that Americans could have settled Polynesia by raft.","The raft stands in its own museum in Oslo, and the 1950 film of the trip won an Academy Award."],"w":"Six men, a balsa raft and one stubborn theory, floated across an ocean to argue about the map of settlement."},{"a":"The Birds of America","al":["birds of america","the birds of america","audubon’s birds of america"],"c":["John James Audubon’s masterwork: 435 life-size engravings of American birds.","Printed between 1827 and 1838 on double-elephant folio sheets about a metre tall.","Life-size meant even the tallest birds folded awkwardly across the great pages.","He wired fresh-killed birds into lively poses rather than painting them as stuffed specimens.","Complete sets now rank among the most expensive printed books ever sold, at well past ten million dollars."],"w":"A whole continent’s birds painted at full scale, in volumes worth more than most houses."},{"a":"The Voyager Golden Record","al":["voyager golden record","the voyager golden record","golden record","voyager record"],"c":["Gold-plated copper records bolted to the two Voyager spacecraft launched in 1977.","A committee under Carl Sagan chose everything on them for any civilisation that might find them.","They carry greetings in fifty-five languages, from ancient Akkadian to Welsh.","The music runs from Bach and Beethoven to Chuck Berry, with whale song and thunder between.","They should outlast the pyramids, drifting intact for perhaps a billion years."],"w":"The human race’s mixtape, thrown at the stars with a needle and instructions, the longest-lived thing we have made."},{"a":"The Pioneer Plaque","al":["pioneer plaque","the pioneer plaque","pioneer 10 plaque","pioneer 11 plaque"],"c":["A small plaque of gold-anodised aluminium bolted to the Pioneer 10 and 11 probes of the early 1970s.","Carl Sagan’s committee designed it as a message to whatever might find the craft.","It shows a naked man and woman, with the silhouette of the ship to give them scale.","A map of pulsars gives the sun’s address in the galaxy, keyed to the spin of the hydrogen atom.","Humanity’s first physical message sent beyond the solar system."],"w":"A gold calling card with a return address in pulsar light, still drifting toward the stars."},{"a":"The Enola Gay","al":["enola gay","the enola gay"],"c":["A US Army Air Forces B-29 bomber, named by its pilot after his mother.","On August 6, 1945, it dropped the uranium bomb on Hiroshima, the first atomic attack in history.","Colonel Paul Tibbets and a crew of eleven flew the mission from the island of Tinian.","The Smithsonian spent two decades restoring it from parts in storage.","It now stands in the museum annex at Dulles Airport, the most argued-about airplane ever built."],"w":"The machine that opened the atomic age in one morning, and a mirror the twentieth century still looks into."},{"a":"The Spruce Goose","al":["spruce goose","the spruce goose","hughes h-4 hercules","h-4 hercules"],"c":["A flying boat with the widest wingspan of its era, built almost entirely of wood.","It was commissioned to fly troops across the Atlantic beyond the reach of submarines.","By the time it was finished the war was over, and Congress called it a waste of birch.","Howard Hughes flew it himself in November 1947, about a mile at seventy feet above Long Beach harbor, its only flight.","It rests today in a museum in Oregon, the trophy of a billionaire’s stubbornness."],"w":"The largest flying boat ever built, aloft once for a minute, famous forever for that minute."},{"a":"The Stanley Cup","al":["stanley cup","the stanley cup","lord stanley’s cup"],"c":["A silver bowl bought in London in 1892 by Lord Stanley of Preston, Canada’s governor general.","The oldest trophy in North American professional sport still competed for.","Every member of the winning club is engraved on it, from superstars to assistant trainers.","When a band fills, the oldest ring is retired to the Hockey Hall of Fame.","Its misspelled names are part of the legend, and it travels everywhere with its own keeper."],"w":"Hockey’s holy grail: a bowl of silver that every player dreams of drinking from."},{"a":"The First Folio","al":["first folio","the first folio","shakespeare first folio","mr william shakespeares comedies histories and tragedies"],"c":["A folio volume of 1623 gathering thirty-six of Shakespeare’s plays, printed seven years after his death.","Two fellow actors, John Heminges and Henry Condell, compiled it so his work would not be lost.","Eighteen of the plays, among them Macbeth and The Tempest, had never been printed before.","About seven hundred and fifty copies were made, and some two hundred and thirty survive.","Without it, half the greatest plays in English would exist only in rumor."],"w":"The book that saved Shakespeare: his friends’ memorial that became the most valuable volume in English."},{"a":"The Exeter Book","al":["exeter book","the exeter book","exeter codex"],"c":["The largest surviving book of Old English poetry, compiled in the tenth century.","Bishop Leofric left it to Exeter Cathedral, where it has stayed for nearly a thousand years.","It holds some ninety riddles, from a bookworm to a storm, some bawdy and some pious.","It also carries The Wanderer, The Seafarer and The Ruin, poems that anchor English literature.","One of the four great Old English codices, and the riddle book of a whole language."],"w":"A tenth-century anthology that keeps half of all surviving Old English verse under one roof."},{"a":"The Vienna Genesis","al":["vienna genesis","the vienna genesis"],"c":["The oldest well-preserved illustrated manuscript of the Bible, made in the sixth century.","Its pages are vellum dyed imperial purple, then as costly as gold.","The Greek text of Genesis is written in silver, blackened now by the centuries.","Forty-eight miniatures run along the bottom of the pages, like a film strip of scripture.","It is kept in the Austrian National Library in Vienna."],"w":"Scripture on imperial purple with pictures: the luxury book of late antiquity, saved whole."},{"a":"The Emerald Buddha","al":["emerald buddha","the emerald buddha","phra kaew morakot"],"c":["The palladium of Thailand: a figure of green jadeite about sixty-six centimetres tall.","Despite the name, there is no emerald in it.","Legend says lightning cracked a stupa in Chiang Rai in 1434 and revealed it hidden inside.","Kings carried it from city to city for centuries before it reached Bangkok in the eighteenth century.","The king of Thailand changes its golden costume at the turn of each of the three seasons."],"w":"A small green stone that kings have warred over, the one object Thailand treats as its soul."},{"a":"The Sword of Joyeuse","al":["sword of joyeuse","the sword of joyeuse","joyeuse","charlemagne’s sword"],"c":["The coronation sword of the kings of France, named the Joyful.","Legend gives it to Charlemagne, and the Song of Roland names a sword of his by this name.","Its actual blade is centuries younger than the emperor, a medieval rebuild.","French kings carried it at their coronations for five hundred years.","It hung in the royal abbey of Saint-Denis until the Revolution, and now hangs in the Louvre."],"w":"The Joyful sword of France: legend of Charlemagne at one end, crown jewellers’ work at the other."},{"a":"The Crown of Saint Wenceslas","al":["crown of saint wenceslas","the crown of saint wenceslas","wenceslas crown","crown of bohemia"],"c":["The crown of the Bohemian kings, made in 1347 for Charles IV.","A little over two kilograms of gold, set with sapphires, rubies, spinels and pearls.","It is kept with the Bohemian coronation jewels inside Saint Vitus’s Cathedral in Prague.","Legend says whoever wears it unlawfully will die within a year.","The tale attached itself even to Reinhard Heydrich, the Nazi protector killed in 1942."],"w":"The crown of Good King Wenceslas, under a curse that has outlived every king who wore it."},{"a":"The Bocksten Man","al":["bocksten man","the bocksten man"],"c":["A bog body dug from a Swedish peat bog in 1936, one of the best-preserved medieval men anywhere.","Two wooden poles were driven through his body to pin him under the peat.","He lived in the fourteenth century and died by violence.","His wool hose, tunic, cloak and hood survive nearly complete, a whole medieval wardrobe.","He wears the most famous haircut of the Middle Ages, long at the back like a modern mullet."],"w":"A murdered man pinned under a bog for six hundred years, wearing the best-preserved medieval clothing on Earth."},{"a":"The Lindow Man","al":["lindow man","the lindow man","pete marsh","lindow bog body"],"c":["A bog body dug from Lindow Moss in Cheshire in 1984.","He died in the Iron Age, about two thousand years ago.","He was killed in stages: a blow to the head, then a cut throat, perhaps a garrote as well.","His stomach held the burned remains of a griddle cake, his last meal.","Wags nicknamed him Pete Marsh, and he lies today in the British Museum."],"w":"A twice-killed man from a Cheshire peat bog, the best witness Britain has to Celtic ritual killing."},{"a":"The Jade Burial Suit","al":["jade burial suit","the jade burial suit","jade suit","jade armor of the han dynasty"],"c":["A full body suit of jade plates from China’s Han dynasty, made for a prince.","Liu Sheng, Prince of Zhongshan, was buried in one, dug from his mountain tomb in 1968.","More than two thousand plates of jade, wired together with gold thread.","Chinese belief held that jade would keep a royal body from decay.","It did not save the prince’s body, but it saved his name."],"w":"A suit of jade and gold built to cheat the grave, two thousand years old and still gapless."},{"a":"The Silla Gold Crown","al":["silla gold crown","the silla gold crown","cheonmachong crown","golden crown of silla"],"c":["A crown of sheet gold from a royal tomb in Gyeongju, Korea, opened in 1973.","Three tree-like prongs and two stag’s antlers rise from its gold band.","Curved beads of jade hang from it in rows, trembling at any movement.","It was made in the Silla kingdom some fifteen hundred years ago.","It stands now in the museum at Gyeongju, the emblem of Korean antiquity."],"w":"A gold tree with antlers, the crown of a Korean kingdom that dressed its kings like sacred groves."},{"a":"The Ram in a Thicket","al":["ram in a thicket","the ram in a thicket","goat in a thicket","ur ram"],"c":["A little goat of gold and lapis, rising on its hind legs to nibble a golden tree.","Made in the city of Ur about four and a half thousand years ago.","Leonard Woolley found a pair of them in a royal death pit in the 1920s.","He named them for the ram caught by its horns in a thicket in the Book of Genesis.","One stands in the British Museum and its twin in Philadelphia."],"w":"Sumer’s goats standing in their golden bushes, dug from a death pit and named from Genesis."},{"a":"The Rongorongo Tablets","al":["rongorongo tablets","the rongorongo tablets","rongorongo","easter island script"],"c":["The undeciphered script of Easter Island, cut into wooden tablets.","Barely two dozen genuine pieces survive, scattered through the world’s museums.","Each line of glyphs runs backwards on alternate rows, plough-fashion, so the reader turns the tablet as he goes.","The islanders lost the art of reading it within a century of European contact, after slave raids and epidemics gutted the population.","No one has read it since, though many have claimed to."],"w":"A whole writing system stranded without a reader: the only indigenous script of Polynesia, and its secret kept."},{"a":"The Vix Krater","al":["vix krater","the vix krater","vix crater","krater of vix"],"c":["The largest metal vessel to survive from the ancient world: 1.63 metres tall, 208 kilograms.","A Greek volute krater for mixing wine, shipped north across the Mediterranean.","It stood in the tomb of a Celtic princess buried at Vix in Burgundy, found in 1953.","The Lady of Vix wore a golden diadem and was buried with a chariot and a great gold torc.","Its frieze of Greek soldiers and chariots marks the far end of a trade route running from Greece to the Celtic heartland."],"w":"A Greek giant standing over a Celtic princess: the clearest proof that Iron Age Europe was one trading world."},{"a":"The Mold Cape","al":["mold cape","the mold cape","mold gold cape"],"c":["A collar of beaten gold in the shape of a short cape, from the Bronze Age.","Quarrymen found it in 1833 under a barrow at Mold in north Wales.","Hammered from a single ingot, its ribs and bosses catch the light like scales.","It was the chest ornament of a person buried with bronze and amber, a priest or a chief.","It is the finest piece of prehistoric goldwork from Wales, kept today in the British Museum."],"w":"A cape of solid light from Bronze Age Wales, beaten from one gold bar four thousand years ago."},{"a":"The Ecstasy of Saint Teresa","al":["ecstasy of saint teresa","the ecstasy of saint teresa","saint teresa in ecstasy","bernini’s teresa"],"c":["Gian Lorenzo Bernini carved it in marble between 1647 and 1652.","It shows Saint Teresa of Avila in the moment of her vision, rapt as a seraph aims a golden dart.","A hidden window pours light down on the pair through rays of gilt wood.","Members of the Cornaro family watch from opera boxes carved into the chapel walls.","It anchors the Cornaro Chapel in the Roman church of Santa Maria della Vittoria, the summit of the Baroque."],"w":"Marble turned to mist and fire: the sculpture where stone stopped being furniture and became theatre."},{"a":"The Coppergate Helmet","al":["coppergate helmet","the coppergate helmet","york helmet","oshere’s helmet"],"c":["An Anglo-Saxon helmet dug from a backyard pit in York in 1982.","Made around 750, of iron with a brass crest ending in an animal head.","A Latin inscription asks the Holy Trinity to protect its owner, named Oshere.","It was found in a pit with other metalwork, cut and battered before burial.","It is the finest Anglian helmet ever found, and sits in York Castle Museum."],"w":"A prayer in iron and brass: the best-preserved helmet of Anglo-Saxon England, dug from a Viking-age street."},{"a":"Newton’s Reflecting Telescope","al":["newton’s telescope","newton’s reflecting telescope","newtonian telescope","newton reflector"],"c":["The reflector Isaac Newton built in 1668 to beat a fault of every lens telescope.","Lenses split starlight into colours; a curved mirror does not, so Newton replaced the lens with polished bronze.","His second little tube, barely six inches long, went to the Royal Society in 1672 and made his name.","The Royal Society still keeps it, the oldest surviving telescope of its kind.","Every backyard dobsonian today is his design, little changed from that drawing."],"w":"The little bronze-barrelled tube that ended two centuries of chromatic smears and gave astronomy its modern eye."},{"a":"The Codex Amiatinus","al":["codex amiatinus","the codex amiatinus"],"c":["The earliest complete Latin Bible in one volume, finished in England in the early eighth century.","Monks of Wearmouth-Jarrow made three of these giant copies; this is the sole survivor.","Its thousand leaves of vellum took the hides of well over a thousand calves.","Abbot Ceolfrith set out to carry it to the pope in Rome and died on the road in 716.","It takes its name from the Tuscan abbey of Monte Amiata, where it rested for centuries before Florence claimed it."],"w":"The oldest complete Latin Bible on Earth: a book too heavy to lift, carried for a thousand miles to a pope."},{"a":"The Gemma Augustea","al":["gemma augustea","the gemma augustea"],"c":["A Roman cameo cut from a slab of two-layered sardonyx, finished around 10 CE.","Above, Augustus sits enthroned with the eagle of Jupiter, and the goddess Roma sits beside him.","Sky, sea and earth send their gods to underwrite his rule.","Below, soldiers of Tiberius raise a victory trophy over captive barbarians.","One of the masterpieces of Roman gem-cutting, kept today in Vienna."],"w":"Imperial propaganda in an inch of layered stone: Augustus as Jupiter, cut by the best hands in Rome."},{"a":"The Book of Durrow","al":["book of durrow","the book of durrow","durrow book"],"c":["An illuminated Gospel book of the seventh century, among the oldest of the great insular books.","Made by monks of the Columban tradition, probably in Ireland or Iona.","Its carpet pages knot and spiral like metalwork, older in feel than anything on the continent.","Each evangelist gets a full-page symbol: a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle.","It has rested at Trinity College Dublin for three hundred years, the forerunner of the Book of Kells."],"w":"The elder sibling of Kells: the first great Gospel book of the Celtic west, still glowing after thirteen centuries."},{"a":"The Americas Cup","al":["americas cup","america’s cup","auld mug"],"c":["A silver ewer first raced for in 1851, when the schooner America beat a fleet of British yachts around the Isle of Wight.","The oldest international sporting trophy still competed for.","Nicknamed the Auld Mug, it was made by a London silversmith in 1848, before it ever had a sport.","The New York Yacht Club defended it for a hundred and thirty-two years.","Australia II finally took it in 1983, and it has crossed oceans ever since."],"w":"The oldest trophy in international sport: a Victorian ewer that nations spend fortunes chasing."}];
